\usepackage[a4paper,margin=27mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{The graph of degree six with one triangle on each edge}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We give an elementary proof that a graph of degree six in which every
edge belongs to exactly one triangle has at least fifteen vertices, and
that equality determines the graph uniquely. The graph at equality is
the Kneser graph on the two-element subsets of a six-element set.
This recovers one previously unresolved uniqueness entry in our audit
of the tables of Goedgebeur and Jooken.
\end{abstract}

\section{Statement and attribution}
All graphs are finite, simple and undirected. The notation
$\operatorname{egr}(n,k,g,\lambda)$ means a connected graph on $n$
vertices, regular of degree $k$, with girth $g$ and exactly $\lambda$
cycles of length $g$ through each edge. Isomorphisms may move every
vertex; no root is distinguished in the classification.

Table~4 of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}, in the fixed edition
\nolinkurl{arXiv:2401.08271v2}, states that the minimum order for
$(k,g,\lambda)=(6,3,1)$ is fifteen and that exactly one graph attains
it. The source table attributes its lower bound to its reference~[8,
Proposition~3.1]. Our previously reviewed lower-bound proof already
recovers that inequality, and the stored example
\nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_44} already supplies existence. The outstanding
entry is the assertion of uniqueness.

We include the short bound again to expose its equality conditions,
then prove uniqueness directly. The resulting classification and the
Kneser graph are established mathematics, not new results claimed by
this project. The contribution here is a self-contained recovery of the
specific table entry, with explicit verification of its identification
with the stored example. We make no claim of historical priority for
the argument or of a solution to an open conjecture.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
A graph of degree six in which every edge belongs to exactly one
triangle has at least fifteen vertices. Up to isomorphism, exactly one
such graph has fifteen vertices: the graph whose vertices are the
two-element subsets of $\{1,\ldots,6\}$, with disjoint subsets adjacent.
In particular, the minimum order of an $\operatorname{egr}(n,6,3,1)$
is fifteen and the number of graphs at that minimum is one.
\end{theorem}

\section{The equality conditions}
For any vertex $x$, every vertex of $N(x)$ has exactly one neighbour
inside $N(x)$: that neighbour is the unique common neighbour of the
endpoints of its edge to $x$. Thus the subgraph on $N(x)$ is a matching
of three edges.

Take a triangle $T=\{a,b,c\}$. Its three neighbourhoods each have size
six. Each pair meets in exactly one vertex, namely the third vertex
of $T$. Their triple intersection is empty, since a vertex adjacent to
all three vertices would give two triangles on an edge of $T$.
Inclusion and exclusion therefore give
\[
 |N(a)\cup N(b)\cup N(c)|=18-3=15.
\]
This proves the lower bound. If the graph has exactly fifteen vertices,
every vertex outside $T$ is adjacent to at least one vertex of $T$.
It cannot be adjacent to two of them, again by uniqueness of the
triangle on an edge of $T$. Consequently:
\begin{equation}\label{one}
 \text{every vertex outside any triangle has exactly one neighbour in it.}
\end{equation}

Fix a vertex $v$. Write the three edges of its neighbourhood as
$u_i^0u_i^1$ for $i=1,2,3$. Let $W$ be the set of the eight vertices
outside $\{v\}\cup N(v)$. Applying \eqref{one} to the triangle
$\{v,u_i^0,u_i^1\}$ shows that each $w\in W$ is adjacent to exactly
one of $u_i^0,u_i^1$. Associate to $w$ the word
\[
 b(w)=(b_1(w),b_2(w),b_3(w))\in\{0,1\}^3,
 \qquad w\sim u_i^{b_i(w)}.
\]
Each $u_i^\epsilon$ has exactly four neighbours in $W$, since its other
two neighbours are $v$ and $u_i^{1-\epsilon}$. Thus each coordinate
of these eight words has four zeroes and four ones, with multiplicity.
Every vertex of $W$ has three neighbours in $W$.

There is no triangle entirely in $W$. For if $x,y,z$ formed one,
\eqref{one} would make each of $u_i^0,u_i^1$ adjacent to exactly one
of $x,y,z$, giving two incidences. But each of $x,y,z$ is adjacent
to one of this pair, giving three incidences, a contradiction.

If $x,y\in W$ are adjacent, their unique common neighbour cannot lie
in $W$, and it cannot be $v$. Their common neighbours in $N(v)$ are
exactly the coordinates in which $b(x)$ and $b(y)$ agree. Hence these
words agree in exactly one coordinate: their Hamming distance is two.
In particular every edge in $W$ preserves the parity of its word.

Each nonempty parity class therefore induces a triangle-free cubic
graph. Such a graph has at least six vertices: the two neighbourhoods
of the endpoints of any edge are disjoint sets of size three. Since
$|W|=8$, only one parity occurs. Exchanging the names $u_1^0,u_1^1$
if necessary makes that parity even.

Let $a,b,c,d$ be the multiplicities of the words $000,011,101,110$,
respectively. The three coordinate balances and the total size give
\[
 a+b=a+c=a+d=4,\qquad a+b+c+d=8.
\]
It follows that $a=b=c=d=2$. Thus $W$ consists of four classes
$W_0,W_1,W_2,W_3$, each of size two, indexed by the even words.

\section{The remaining edges}
There is no edge inside one word class: its endpoints would have
three common neighbours in $N(v)$. Two distinct even words agree in
exactly one coordinate, say the value $\epsilon$ at coordinate $i$.
The four vertices in the corresponding two word classes are precisely
the neighbours in $W$ of $u_i^\epsilon$.

The neighbourhood of $u_i^\epsilon$ induces three disjoint edges.
One of these is $vu_i^{1-\epsilon}$. Neither of these two vertices is
adjacent to the four vertices under consideration: $v$ has no neighbour
in $W$, and those four words have coordinate $\epsilon$, rather than
$1-\epsilon$. The remaining four vertices therefore induce two disjoint
edges. Since there are no edges inside a word class, these two edges
form a perfect matching between the classes.

It remains to choose, for each of the six pairs of classes, one of the
two possible perfect matchings. Label the two vertices of $W_0$ by
$0,1$. Label each other class so that its matching with $W_0$ joins
equal labels. For distinct $i,j\in\{1,2,3\}$, the matching between
$W_i$ and $W_j$ must then join opposite labels. An equal-label
matching would give a triangle together with a vertex of $W_0$.
This determines all six matchings uniquely. The three remaining
opposite-label matchings create no triangle among $W_1,W_2,W_3$,
because three changes of a binary label cannot return to its starting
value. All edges of the original graph have now been determined,
up to the choices of names already made. There is at most one
isomorphism class at order fifteen.

To prove existence and identify this class, take the fifteen
two-element subsets of a six-element set, joining two when they
are disjoint. Each vertex has $\binom42=6$ neighbours. For adjacent
vertices, their union has four elements, and their only common
neighbour is the remaining two-element subset. Triangles exist by
partitioning the six-element set into three pairs. The graph is
connected: two distinct intersecting pairs have a common disjoint
pair in the three-element complement of their union. It has the
required parameters, which proves Theorem~\ref{main}.

\section{Verification and scope}
The accompanying elementary verifier constructs the last graph directly
and checks its degree, connectedness and every edge's triangle count.
It checks a literal bijection to the stored graph from the authors'
repository. It also tests all $2^6=64$ choices of the six matchings
above. Exactly eight choices have no triangle; the eight changes
of labels in the last three classes identify all of them with the
unique normalized choice. This small finite check illustrates the
last step of the proof; the classification itself is supplied by the
argument, not by assuming a census of fifteen-vertex graphs.

This note completes one numerical uniqueness entry. It neither
completes the recovery of all tables of the original nor supplies
a new Lean formalization or a compression measurement.

